# Fastah geofeed quality analyzer

The first schema-first vertical slice of `tuning-geofeeds`: a local RFC 8805
CSV file becomes typed Pydantic models, deterministic findings and prefix
relationships, versioned analysis JSON, optional authoritative RDAP evidence,
host-mediated Fastah place-search evidence, and an explicit correction
proposal/approval/export workflow. IR-only renderers produce Markdown, a
portable HTML dashboard, and a privacy-allowlisted GeoJSON projection.

RDAP is the only network stage implemented by the analyzer. Marketplace hosts
or agents—not this package—discover, authenticate to, and invoke Fastah MCP.
This implementation does not provide remote feed download. It now generates
private Amp, Claude, OpenAI review, and public-export staging layouts without
publishing them. See [`docs/`](docs/) for specifications.

## Setup and checks

Python 3.13 or newer is required. On Windows, we recommend installing Python
through [Python Install Manager in the Microsoft Store](https://apps.microsoft.com/detail/9nq7512cxl7t?hl=en-US)
so it receives automatic updates; other installation methods remain supported.

```bash
make setup
make check             # Ruff, mypy, schema drift, pytest
make schema-generate   # intentionally refresh schema/analysis.schema.json
make schema-check      # fail when models and committed schema differ
# Explicit, non-CI live smoke (contacts IANA and an authoritative RIR)
make test-live-rdap
```

## Analyze and render

```bash
make analyze INPUT=tests/fixtures/valid.csv OUTPUT=/tmp/analysis.json
make render INPUT=/tmp/analysis.json OUTPUT=/tmp/analysis.md
make render-html INPUT=/tmp/analysis.json OUTPUT=/tmp/dashboard.html
make export-geojson INPUT=/tmp/analysis.json OUTPUT=/tmp/analysis.geojson
make mcp-export INPUT=/tmp/analysis.json BATCH_LIMIT=1000 OUTPUT_DIR=/tmp/mcp-requests
make mcp-import INPUT=/tmp/analysis.json \
  RESPONSES='/tmp/mcp-response-1.json' MAPPING=/tmp/mcp-requests/mapping.json \
  BATCH_LIMIT=1000 \
  OUTPUT=/tmp/analysis-with-mcp.json

# Equivalent installed CLI
geofeed-quality analyze feed.csv --output analysis.json
geofeed-quality render analysis.json --output analysis.md
geofeed-quality render-html analysis.json --output dashboard.html
geofeed-quality export-geojson analysis.json --output analysis.geojson
geofeed-quality schema check
```

## Explicit correction approval and CSV export

Correction generation, human/host decisions, and export are separate. The
initial conservative rules propose deterministic country/region case and
whitespace normalization, deterministic removal of a populated deprecated
postal-code value, and advisory best-first MCP country/region/city suggestions.
MCP suggestions use `not_assessed`; rank, population weight, and radius are not
confidence. RDAP never proposes a location change.

```bash
geofeed-quality propose-corrections analysis.json \
  --output analysis-proposed.json --plan correction-plan.json

# Read correction-plan.json, then name every explicit decision. No action is defaulted.
geofeed-quality record-approval correction-plan.json \
  --approver "review-session-7" --decided-at "2026-08-15T12:30:00Z" \
  --approve proposal-0123456789abcdef --reject proposal-fedcba9876543210 \
  --output correction-approval.json

geofeed-quality export-csv analysis-proposed.json correction-approval.json \
  --source feed.csv --output corrected.csv --final-analysis analysis-final.json
```

Export requires at least one explicit `approve` decision. Approval binds the
source digest, analysis/schema identity, complete proposal-set digest,
user/host-supplied approver label, and user/host-supplied timestamp. The
exporter verifies the original source bytes, reanalyzes the candidate output,
then writes a new CSV and finalized Analysis atomically without overwriting an
existing path or source. Comments, blank lines, UTF-8 BOM/content, physical row
order, authored four/five/extension-column shape, and CRLF/LF/CR delimiters are
retained; only approved data rows are safely reserialized.

## HTML and GeoJSON visualization

Both visualization commands accept only a serialized, schema-valid Analysis
IR. They never read CSV or invoke RDAP or MCP. The portable dashboard embeds
the validated IR, privacy-filtered GeoJSON, local Sora font, Fastah logo, CSS,
and JavaScript in one deterministic file. Without Mapbox configuration it
makes no network requests and provides a searchable, paginated row table plus
textual geographic evidence.

Mapbox is an explicit enhancement. Put a restricted public `pk.*` token in a
UTF-8 file, then select a `mapbox://styles/<owner>/<style>` or equivalent
Mapbox Styles API URL:

```bash
geofeed-quality render-html analysis.json --output dashboard.html \
  --mapbox-token-file /secure/path/mapbox-public-token.txt \
  --mapbox-style mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12
```

The configured dashboard necessarily contains that public token; the analyzer
never writes it to Analysis JSON, GeoJSON, or Markdown and never logs it. Use
URL restrictions and least-privilege scopes. A URL-restricted token requires
serving the dashboard from an allowed HTTP(S) origin rather than opening it as
`file://`. A missing token, blocked network, or style failure leaves the full
offline dashboard and textual geography usable.

Offline CSP is `default-src 'none'`, content hashes for inline scripts/data and
CSS, `font-src data:`, `img-src data:`, and no connect/worker/frame/object/form
targets. Mapbox mode additionally allows pinned GL JS script/style resources at
`https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v3.28.1/`, inline SDK-generated style,
`worker-src blob:`, `img-src data: blob:`, and connections only to
`https://api.mapbox.com` and `https://events.mapbox.com`. This follows the
[Mapbox GL JS CSP guidance](https://docs.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/guides/security-and-testing/);
the renderer disables optional performance metrics collection.

GeoJSON includes only existing best-match MCP points and bounding boxes. Its
properties allowlist contains row ID, canonical prefix, advisory place/status,
and finding summary fields. Creating this user-chosen artifact therefore
discloses prefixes, but omits source URL/comments, publisher/RDAP identifiers,
raw MCP messages, unapproved corrections, radius, rank, and population weight.
Both outputs attribute GeoNames; Mapbox mode retains Mapbox GL JS attribution.

Dashboard design sources are
[`design/tokens/colors.css`](../../design/tokens/colors.css),
[`design/tokens/typography.css`](../../design/tokens/typography.css),
[`design/tokens/spacing.css`](../../design/tokens/spacing.css),
[`design/tokens/radius.css`](../../design/tokens/radius.css),
[`design/components/data/StatCard.jsx`](../../design/components/data/StatCard.jsx),
[`design/components/core/Card.jsx`](../../design/components/core/Card.jsx),
[`design/components/core/Badge.jsx`](../../design/components/core/Badge.jsx),
[`design/assets/fonts/Sora-VariableFont_wght.ttf`](../../design/assets/fonts/Sora-VariableFont_wght.ttf),
and [`design/assets/logos/fastah-lockup-ondark.svg`](../../design/assets/logos/fastah-lockup-ondark.svg).

## Host-mediated Fastah MCP exchange

The host discovers `rfc8805-row-place-search`, supplies its advertised batch
limit, invokes each exported `{rows}` object, and captures the structured
contract `1.0` response. Exported rows contain only opaque `rowId`, `country`,
optional `region`, optional `city`, and `searchMode`. They contain no prefix,
source metadata, publisher profile, or RDAP evidence. The host transport
deduplicates only exact `(country, region, city, searchMode)` tuples in
first-seen order. A versioned, integrity-bound `mapping.json` remains local and
fans each representative response back to one observation per source row.

```bash
geofeed-quality mcp-export analysis.json \
  --batch-limit 1000 --output-dir mcp-requests
geofeed-quality mcp-import analysis.json mcp-response-1.json \
  --mapping mcp-requests/mapping.json \
  --batch-limit 1000 --output analysis-with-mcp.json
```

Use the limit reported by host discovery; 1,000 is only the current server
value. Request files contain location tuples but no network identity and need
no credential or persistent intermediary secret. Import validates the complete
response contract and adds advisory observations/findings without changing
source rows, base findings, or correction state. Matches remain best-first;
rank, `populationWeightPercent`, and `approximateRadiusKm` are not confidence.

## Optional authoritative RDAP evidence

Base analysis remains offline unless `--rdap` is present. The optional profile
is a JSON object containing any combination of `organization_name`, `asn`,
`rdap_entity_handle`, `rir_organization_id`, and `domain`:

```bash
geofeed-quality analyze feed.csv --output analysis.json \
  --rdap --publisher-profile publisher.json
```

The client downloads current IPv4 and IPv6 bootstrap data from IANA, selects
the longest matching service, and contacts only credential-free HTTPS RIR
endpoints. Defaults are 5-second connect and 10-second read timeouts, a 1 MiB
response limit, three redirects, two workers, and 0.5 seconds between request
starts per RIR. CLI flags prefixed `--rdap-` configure those operational
bounds. The per-run in-memory cache is discarded on exit.

The IR retains the returned network range/handle and only registrant entity
handles, organization names, roles, and allowlisted public identifiers needed
for comparison. It never retains full vCards, email addresses, phone numbers,
authorization headers, or unrelated response fields. Assessments are exactly
`consistent`, `conflicting`, `unverified`, or `unavailable`; none establishes
legal ownership.

`render` accepts only a serialized analysis IR. It does not accept CSV and does
not import the parser or analyzer. Input JSON is schema-validated and parsed
into the typed root model before rendering.

## Current contract decisions

- Schema version: `0.4.0`; enum version: `4`.
- Finding categories are independent from severities (`error`, `warning`,
  `info`).
- Every physical line is retained. Initial UTF-8 BOM is accepted and recorded;
  invalid UTF-8 is a typed feed-level error.
- Single host addresses normalize to `/32` or `/128`. CIDR strings with host
  bits set are retained but invalid under rule `RFC8805.PREFIX_HOST_BITS`.
- Full-prefix public-routability is a Fastah quality policy, not RFC validity.
- Exact/equal-prefix relationships use a deterministic star, and containment
  links only immediate parents and carved children. This bounds graph growth
  linearly at the supported 60,000-row limit.

## Agent Skill and evaluations

The canonical private Agent Skill is
[`tuning-geofeeds/SKILL.md`](tuning-geofeeds/SKILL.md). It uses the bundled
analyzer commands rather than generating parser or renderer code. Setup and
interpretation details are progressively disclosed through its `references/`
directory; canonical prompts, fixtures, deterministic code graders, and human
dashboard feedback fields are under `evals/`.

```bash
make skill-validate       # metadata, references, release inventory, secrets, portable smoke
make skill-eval           # 16 fixture cases; generated evidence remains gitignored
make skill-release-check  # deterministic private portable release layout
make skill-package-check  # host views, real Amp install, deterministic packaging
```

The checked-in `tuning-geofeeds/` directory is the development source and is
not directly installable. In particular, pointing `amp skill add` at that
directory copies only `SKILL.md`. Build the canonical repository-shaped bundle
and point Amp at its parent root so all scripts, references, analyzer code,
schemas, fixtures, and design assets survive installation:

```bash
make skill-package-build OUTPUT=/path/to/new/fastah-netops-tools-release
amp skill add --target /path/to/isolated/skills \
  /path/to/new/fastah-netops-tools-release/canonical

# Explicit staging for the later fastah/ip-geofeed-skills export; no push occurs.
make skill-public-stage OUTPUT=/path/to/new/public-stage
```

The release builder fails when output exists. `canonical/tuning-geofeeds/` is
the single distributable skill bundle; Amp, Claude, and OpenAI views are exact
copies tied to its digest. Claude uses `.claude-plugin/plugin.json`; OpenAI
review material uses `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, a tool-filtered `.mcp.json`,
reviewer cases, CSP inventory, and a blocker-aware readiness report. The
allowlisted trees are credential-free and include the exact Design Library
font/logo. Public push and marketplace submission remain separately approved
future actions.
